data.content:
## What breaks in .NET 11

In EF Core for .NET 9, the Cosmos DB provider **removed synchronous I/O support** — sync methods like `ToList()`, `First()`, `SaveChanges()` throw `InvalidOperationException` because the underlying Cosmos SDK is async-only. A temporary **opt-in** (`EnableSyncOverAsync`) let you keep sync calls working in .NET 9/10.

In .NET 11, that opt-in is **removed entirely**. Every sync call in your repository will throw at runtime.

### What to fix

Convert all sync calls to their async equivalents:

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

### Key changes

| Sync (breaks) | Async replacement |
|---|---|
| `ToList()` | `await ToListAsync()` |
| `First()` | `await FirstAsync()` |
| `Count()` | `await CountAsync()` |
| `SaveChanges()` | `await SaveChangesAsync()` |

You'll also need to propagate `async/await` up through all callers (controllers, services, etc.). Remove the `EnableSyncOverAsync` configuration since it no longer exists.

> **Note:** .NET 11 is not yet released — this is based on the announced deprecation trajectory. Verify against the official migration guide when it ships.
